2042:
From the hour of nine-thirty o'clock a.m. (9:30 a.m.) to the hour of nine-thirty o'clock p.m. (9:30 p.m.), it shall be unlawful for the operator of any motor vehicle having a trailer or other vehicle attached thereto, to load or unload goods, wares or merchandise, or other articles or materials, or to stop such motor vehicle for the purpose of loading or unloading goods, wares or merchandise, or other articles or materials, on the following parts of streets in the city, to-wit:
(1) Broadway, from the north line of Ninth Street to the south line of Fourteenth Street;
(2) First Avenue, from the north line of Ninth Street to the north line of Fourteenth Street;
(3) Tenth Street, from the west line of Second Avenue to the west line of Broadway;
(4) Eleventh Street, from the west line of Second Avenue to the west line of Broadway;
(5) Twelfth Street, from the west line of Second Avenue to the west line of Broadway;
(6) Thirteenth Street, from the west line of Second Avenue to the west line of Broadway.
(Ord. No. 71-198, § 16, 7-20-71)

2103:
(1) Before issuing any driver permit to a taxicab driver applicant, the chief of police shall make a background check of the applicant to determine whether or not the applicant has a criminal record.
(2) No permit shall be issued nor renewed to any applicant who has:
(a) Within the last five years received a conviction for a federal or state felony.
(b) Within the last three years received a conviction for misdemeanor or ordinance violation relating to immoral purposes to include but not limited to:
1. Prostitution;
2. Soliciting for prostitution;
3. Any sex related offense;
4. Alcohol offense;
5. Drug offense;
6. Fraud; and
7. Gambling.
(c) Within the last three years received a conviction for any of the following offenses (unless the five year felony rule applies):
1. DUI;
2. Reckless driving;
3. Hit and run;
4. Vehicular homicide;
5. Serious injury by vehicle;
6. Suspended driver's license;
7. Road rage; and,
8. Overcharging a passenger.
(d) Within one year of the application for a driver's permit received a conviction for either bad checks or four or more moving traffic violations under either state or local or federal law.
(Ord. No. 04-69, § 1, 8-10-04)
